#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175679 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/id #uuid "7a8775a6-f16c-5fc8-865b-89728d73a2c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-213" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t) = -\\frac{1}{3}t^3 + \\frac{5}{2}t^2 + 6t + 1,\\quad t\\geq 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary, and give the displacement of the particle from the origin at this time." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}+6t+1) \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}+5t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\, \\textrm{m/s}$. So, we set $v = 0$ and solve for $t \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-{t}^{2}+5t+6 \\\\ 0 & ={t}^{2}-5t-6 \\\\ 0 & =(t-6)(t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=6$ and $t=-1.$ Since $t\\geq 0,$ we discard the solution $t=-1$ and keep only $t=6.$\n\nSo the particle is stationary only at $t=6\\, \\textrm{s}.$ To find the displacement from the origin at this time, we substitute $t=6$ back into $s(t)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {6}^{3}+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}+6\\cdot 6+1 \\\\  & =-72+90+36+1 \\\\  & =55\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t = 6\\, \\textrm{s}$ and its displacement from the origin at this time is $55\\, \\textrm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175680 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "a6a4547d-a81a-5c9a-9ab1-2bb20d847fca" :question/math-academy-id "e-217" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t) = t^3-3t^2+12t,\\quad t\\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+12t) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-6t+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-6t+12) \\\\  & =6t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =6t-6 \\\\ 6t & =6 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1$. To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1) = 3(1)^2 - 6(1)+12 = 9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175712 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/id #uuid "95ddf955-ea59-5ac8-977d-767e46b92bf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6159" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t) = t^4 - 32t$, where $t \\geq 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-32t) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t) <    0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3}-32 & <0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3} & <32 \\\\ {t}^{3} & <8 \\\\ t & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in\\left[ 0, 2\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8cd8a91-5381-568a-bbfe-1f703ad6407d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293114} {:answer/id #uuid "b40340f9-c466-514f-884a-2e7d7b67a2de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2{e}^{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293115} {:answer/id #uuid "4b609f51-6620-5550-84a4-565df43107d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3{e}^{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293116} {:answer/id #uuid "59bf99a4-4487-5b03-b2c0-5ba248cdffc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293117} {:answer/id #uuid "49a8aa83-c4a2-589e-842a-b85f4529b97f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59bf99a4-4487-5b03-b2c0-5ba248cdffc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293117} :answer-field/id #uuid "35640171-26d5-56b7-8531-903070905429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25e83974-7897-5d7f-96f2-7e49663e3f50" :question/math-academy-id "q-109274" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=(t-3){e}^{t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-3){e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+(t-3){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-2){e}^{t} & =0 \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4bb3f7f-3469-5405-897c-ae2bd7b7985a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293121} {:answer/id #uuid "713f8e69-adfe-5c71-91a2-a882cc7523a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293122} {:answer/id #uuid "53df5b51-d99c-5a79-819b-3a94533a12c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293123} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5fdd4f-b41c-552d-8974-8eaffca85c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293124} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6ec469-cd59-593b-bc0a-a723d4abd25f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4bb3f7f-3469-5405-897c-ae2bd7b7985a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293121} :answer-field/id #uuid "b14b2892-93f5-53db-aabb-53470c6190ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2e365c2-fe58-52a7-96c4-f4db359dbf61" :question/math-academy-id "q-109275" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}-6t+10),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}-6t+10)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-6}{{t}^{2}-6t+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t-3)}{{t}^{2}-6t+10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(t-3)}{{t}^{2}-6t+10} & =0 \\\\ 2(t-3) & =0 \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a21dfbdd-deb0-5415-a30b-f58eaa37838a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293128} {:answer/id #uuid "1b591117-c147-5078-bb26-0bc06b59aa5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=e\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293129} {:answer/id #uuid "91f98767-8bd1-536f-9465-8fe19115b782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\sqrt{e}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293130} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcbc8b2-e2e2-5466-a60b-ff17c0cc6836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{e}}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293131} {:answer/id #uuid "846c852f-b19d-5eb0-9c5f-6a43fa64007d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "846c852f-b19d-5eb0-9c5f-6a43fa64007d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293132} :answer-field/id #uuid "3130adfc-ef2a-5b80-b47c-71c848fca0b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d098c0db-a314-5213-9701-dea3457fb68f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109276" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t \\\\  & =t(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1) & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de7a8620-0953-5a2a-99bd-578e05b45ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293135} {:answer/id #uuid "bd259a28-0f07-56f4-99b3-cc005d451f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293136} {:answer/id #uuid "cf33ce5c-8b17-5a2b-b843-cd05b9188fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293137} {:answer/id #uuid "9aaa5172-9aef-55df-9812-6f0aa569256a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293138} {:answer/id #uuid "36244f2c-51b5-5da6-861d-f5d5cf3e1a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd259a28-0f07-56f4-99b3-cc005d451f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293136} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dc3cc59-7e89-5c2c-824f-cbadf0f09f87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6414dac4-9443-5e38-b246-ae2ca315e478" :question/math-academy-id "q-109302" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=2{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}+3t-2,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}+3t-2) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-18t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-18t+3) \\\\  & =12t-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12t-18 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{18}{12} \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{3}{2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{3}{2}) & =6{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-18(\\frac{3}{2})+3 \\\\  & =6(\\frac{9}{4})-27+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-27+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbf2036c-e6a0-56af-9ea3-9f5e80a00d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293142} {:answer/id #uuid "07ed0936-e17d-56a2-839c-782eb9417875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4,\\,v=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293143} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb12910-836c-5ee3-a2d4-8227b04bd969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293144} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6707f7-9d76-52fc-9d3a-9585efc9456b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4,\\,v=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293145} {:answer/id #uuid "3797d18a-9760-5eed-b498-d2249d5f5bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07ed0936-e17d-56a2-839c-782eb9417875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4,\\,v=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293143} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2b6a8bf-a6eb-53f0-87b5-74af3210b40e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c55e4cb-31ae-5f26-9005-765720eabd10" :question/math-academy-id "q-109303" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=\\frac{{t}^{3}}{2}-6{t}^{2}+30t+4,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{2}-6{t}^{2}+30t+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}-12t+30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}-12t+30) \\\\  & =3t-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3t-12 & =0 \\\\ 3t & =12 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the acceleration is zero when $t=4.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(4) & =\\frac{3(4)^{2}}{2}-12(4)+30 \\\\  & =24-48+30 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd79ef94-fcdf-58f6-aa30-5aa511cb311b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd79ef94-fcdf-58f6-aa30-5aa511cb311b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293149} :answer-field/id #uuid "21660576-1240-5665-a5f3-e25ef9579462" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293148} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2110c1da-b1a4-56c2-839d-ec6245598cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2110c1da-b1a4-56c2-839d-ec6245598cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293151} :answer-field/id #uuid "45b0bf94-741a-59b3-b780-9aa3ef6a0c61" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c26d8c3c-e107-52ba-89a9-ccc2af1eaed1" :question/math-academy-id "q-315550" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=(4t-7){e}^{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((4t-7){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}+(4t-7){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t} & >0 \\\\ 4t-3 & >0 \\\\ t & >\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of increasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e13c911-464c-5d7f-8dcd-8a6399f99780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e13c911-464c-5d7f-8dcd-8a6399f99780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293154} :answer-field/id #uuid "16c17a4d-4139-5a95-bd2c-8dcaaaf8d895" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293153} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4abb84c0-11c0-517e-990b-238a48e3c5a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4abb84c0-11c0-517e-990b-238a48e3c5a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293156} :answer-field/id #uuid "2890d096-a837-580f-a80b-fcad1fdec5ef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92d00e03-01ad-583d-a312-9474b7c65d31" :question/math-academy-id "q-335891" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}-20t+29},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}-20t+29}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10t-20}{2\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}-20t+29}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(t-2)}{2\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}-20t+29}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(t-2)}{\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}-20t+29}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ \\frac{5(t-2)}{\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}-20t+29}} & >0 \\\\ 5(t-2) & >0 \\\\ t-2 & >0 \\\\ t & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of increasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (2,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b799486a-3a34-5012-8eb2-40d2060c035a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b799486a-3a34-5012-8eb2-40d2060c035a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293159} :answer-field/id #uuid "9883c0c2-64e6-59f0-a592-6d80b6d75371" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293158} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46b58199-f9d9-567b-bfaf-99cd1a368168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46b58199-f9d9-567b-bfaf-99cd1a368168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293161} :answer-field/id #uuid "38e1fde2-d1f3-5faa-8af2-8a854a4a24d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a057f4ca-b8df-55b8-b000-d7d91fd329b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335906" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{5}+5t,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5}+5t) \\\\  & =5{t}^{4}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{4}+5) \\\\  & =20{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a(t)=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the acceleration is zero when $t=0.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =5(0)^{4}+5 \\\\  & =0+5 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=0,$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c528b48f-fdbf-5397-b017-50d5926ff5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=7\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293164} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8c6717-d882-5d52-a491-71cffae3080e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=8\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293165} {:answer/id #uuid "0bfa9dab-4042-5177-b960-f74d772f577f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293166} {:answer/id #uuid "ec27b930-4e71-59aa-b104-86b82ce1b543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293167} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe38831-2ebc-538b-a2c2-50cfd4926f08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=10\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bfa9dab-4042-5177-b960-f74d772f577f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293166} :answer-field/id #uuid "d05e1821-bf10-52dd-b786-afb9afa67640" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24413cc5-0267-5920-b89f-10f793534ebf" :question/math-academy-id "q-4771" :question/problem "The position $x(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2}-10t-2,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-10t-2) \\\\  & =2t-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-10 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =10 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516e0e63-71c6-55ed-97c4-7de922ca8d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,+\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293171} {:answer/id #uuid "38fc2c1d-45af-5e1c-aaa8-aa3aef0a9c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293172} {:answer/id #uuid "0e0a6517-4be9-5455-8d1e-f73ee43cab39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(4,8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293173} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1f4886-e014-579a-85eb-ab6560fee7d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293174} {:answer/id #uuid "a066c75f-3bc7-51a4-8343-3ad5b2be2feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[0,1)$ and $(2,+\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38fc2c1d-45af-5e1c-aaa8-aa3aef0a9c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293172} :answer-field/id #uuid "966fbba2-e395-5db4-80cc-399e442cbf30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c0cff5d-5154-58d2-b27e-3d3d4bba184f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48911" :question/problem "The displacement $s$ (in meters) relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)={e}^{{t}^{2}-4t+6}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative.\n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{{t}^{2}-4t+6}) \\\\  & =(2t-4){e}^{{t}^{2}-4t+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we must solve $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (2t-4){e}^{{t}^{2}-4t+6} & <0 \\\\ 2t-4 & <0 \\\\ 2t & <4 \\\\ t & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $[0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b1fbde-50c2-5d53-b5a1-85d5f81fba1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293178} {:answer/id #uuid "a58f6864-c59b-5bdc-b124-ec8f5452cad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293179} {:answer/id #uuid "fee1eebf-f311-55a9-9222-2595f1919cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[0,\\frac{5}{3})$ and $(\\frac{7}{2},\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293180} {:answer/id #uuid "21a8c3c8-b06e-51dc-8f8f-6f18677bbf62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{13}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293181} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6afbe4-d16e-5d05-9fc4-d7aab17e979a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{5}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23b1fbde-50c2-5d53-b5a1-85d5f81fba1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293178} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b20ae87-2caa-5a0e-81f0-bb93426cd586" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc288872-1b0f-5431-bcdb-4e1ae6d59811" :question/math-academy-id "q-48912" :question/problem "The displacement $s$ (in meters) relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=(4t-7){e}^{t}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive.\n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((4t-7){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =(4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t} & >0 \\\\ 4t-3 & >0 \\\\ t & >\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of increasing $x$ is $(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "559aca53-c76f-5fe2-9796-05e478a143a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4,\\,v=10.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293185} {:answer/id #uuid "74745f3f-0369-5e06-ae45-e46284728b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4.5,\\,v=27.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293186} {:answer/id #uuid "6c794664-d4be-5c42-8d2e-8b38f7bc2221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4.5,\\,v=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293187} {:answer/id #uuid "029a872a-a5b9-50b1-855f-8c9f9a283eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3.5,\\,v=26.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293188} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f415dc-47c2-5c64-8808-2e0040849906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4,\\,v=22.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74745f3f-0369-5e06-ae45-e46284728b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4.5,\\,v=27.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293186} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b117898-dfe1-5e92-bcc9-e5e138747efe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e823f661-78ed-5c49-931f-9f7b092bd2fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-7188" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=-\\frac{2}{9}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+14t+17,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{2}{9}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+14t+17) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}+6t+14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}+6t+14) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\frac{4}{3}t+6 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}t & =6 \\\\ t & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=4.5.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(4.5)=-\\frac{2}{3}(4.5)^{2}+6(4.5)+14=27.5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64a51385-11d7-51c7-a2d4-7659fb60ca51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,s=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293192} {:answer/id #uuid "e5251bd4-427b-5273-8a5d-2b9528d5d26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3,\\,s=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293193} {:answer/id #uuid "9ceb25f5-097c-520e-aaf4-5ab806fd9f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3,\\,s=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293194} {:answer/id #uuid "55b41eab-0494-56bc-92fe-c4dd35e7ce67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,s=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293195} {:answer/id #uuid "7b258107-4669-57fb-b219-823bab2c3396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,s=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55b41eab-0494-56bc-92fe-c4dd35e7ce67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,s=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293195} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d351ef-39f4-5dae-94d8-3aeaa48f46a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2ee9a7a-96f8-5c1a-98bc-31a0726aad61" :question/math-academy-id "q-73188" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}-2t+8,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time when the particle is stationary, and give the displacement of the particle from the origin at this time." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+8) \\\\  & =2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-2 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =2 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the particle is stationary only at $t=1.$ To find the displacement from the origin at this time, we substitute $t=1$ back into $s(t)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(1) & ={1}^{2}-2(1)+8 \\\\  & =1-2+8 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=1$ and its displacement from the origin at this time is $7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c75026-69c0-5fd1-931c-66bcbf46941e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293199} {:answer/id #uuid "24e3583f-547f-5fd4-a804-1deccb1ec905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293200} {:answer/id #uuid "36b83a65-b648-5c45-964c-a215cbb65863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293201} {:answer/id #uuid "b30f0709-5e9b-57ba-b7eb-3c77da20fffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293202} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d034a0-68f3-5d2b-988f-e11655a10068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b30f0709-5e9b-57ba-b7eb-3c77da20fffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293202} :answer-field/id #uuid "adf9aa9a-aa1a-51c7-baeb-c45e3854e95c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08503449-abde-5746-b6d7-46180193efed" :question/math-academy-id "q-73210" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=t{e}^{-2t}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2t}(1-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ {e}^{-2t}(1-2t) & >0 \\\\ 1-2t & >0 \\\\ 2t & <1 \\\\ t & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of increasing $x$ is $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3642872-9487-5302-8cd6-43314b0b78cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = e^{-3/2}, v =-2e^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175459} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ad42bb-06cc-5675-9192-87736ec8566e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = e^{-5/2}, v =-e^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175460} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cdb155-39ad-5ef3-9905-e8e0a468e6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = e^{-3/2}, v =-e^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175461} {:answer/id #uuid "3354c4fb-c827-5c6e-adb4-d04af78f599a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = e^{-1/2}, v =-2e^{-1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175462} {:answer/id #uuid "6241a30d-ffed-548d-988f-d84d4b829de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = e^{-5/2}, v =-2e^{-5/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3642872-9487-5302-8cd6-43314b0b78cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = e^{-3/2}, v =-2e^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175459} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8d9b94d-adb8-5a4a-93bd-d684f585cbb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4d760c3-ed2b-5b02-959e-0a9b0b6ecac1" :question/math-academy-id "q-73334" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+3 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ t & ={e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t={e}^{-3/2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v({e}^{-3/2}) & =2({e}^{-3/2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-3/2})+{e}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3/2}(-\\frac{3}{2})+{e}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
